
  The AIX port supports AIX 4.x and 5.x and provides these meters:

    - load meter
    - cpu meter:  user | system | io-wait | idle
    - mem meter:  used | cache | free
    - swap meter: used | free
    - page meter: paging space traffic, in | out | idle
    - disk meter: read | write | idle
    - net meter:  in | out | idle

  Building
  --------

    cp targets/aix5 .config        # AIX 5.1 and later
    cp targets/aix4 .config        # AIX 4.1 through 4.3
    gmake

  The build needs GNU make; the AIX make will not parse the Makefile.  It
  needs no libraries beyond libX11, and in particular does not need
  libXpm, which AIX does not ship: the targets define -DNO_XPM, which
  drops support for the pixmapName resource and leaves everything else
  intact.

  Where the numbers come from
  ---------------------------

  The meters do not read any statistic directly.  They go through the
  small interface in aix/aixstats.h, which has two implementations; the
  target file picks one by setting AIXSTATS.

  aix/perfstat.cc is the AIX 5.x back end and the default.  It uses
  libperfstat, which arrived in AIX 5.1 and is the supported interface
  for all of these figures.  It needs no special privileges and its
  counters are 64 bit, so they do not wrap.

  aix/kmem.cc is the AIX 4.x back end, for the releases that predate
  libperfstat.  It reads everything out of /dev/kmem at addresses
  resolved with knlist().  The symbols used are avenrun (load averages),
  sysinfo (cpu ticks, see <sys/sysinfo.h>), vmminfo (paging counters, see
  <sys/vminfo.h>), ifnet (the interface chain) and iostat (the per disk
  dkstat chain, see <sys/iostat.h>).

  Real memory and paging space totals have no header on AIX 4.x and come
  from the kernel "vmker" symbol instead.  The layout in aix/vmker.h was
  reverse engineered by Jussi Maki for "monitor" and is the same one the
  AIX 4.1 module of top(1) uses.  It has been checked against "lsattr -El
  sys0 -a realmem", the vmstat "fre" column and lsps(1).  Note that this
  layout is specific to AIX 4.x: the same symbol exists on AIX 5.1 but
  its fields have moved, which is one reason the 5.x back end uses
  libperfstat rather than extending the guesswork.

  Because the 4.x figures are kernel internals rather than a supported
  interface, they are the most likely thing to need attention on a
  release other than the ones tested.  Each meter checks its statistic at
  startup and disables itself if it is unavailable, so an unsupported
  kernel loses individual meters rather than failing to start.

  Notes
  -----

  The disk meter reports the same counters as iostat(1), and the kernel
  only maintains those when disk i/o history is turned on.  If the disk
  meter stays flat and "iostat -d" prints "Disk history since boot not
  available", enable it with:

    chdev -l sys0 -a iostat=true

  The page meter counts traffic to and from paging space only, which is
  what vmstat reports in its pi and po columns.  Ordinary file paging is
  deliberately left out because the disk meter already covers it.

  The cpu meter is a single aggregate meter.  The ticks both back ends
  read are accumulated system wide, so the cpuFormat resource has no
  effect on this platform.

  The mem meter shows the file cache separately from used memory, since
  AIX will hand those pages back under memory pressure.

  On AIX 4.x the interface and disk byte counters are 32 bit and will
  wrap on a busy machine.  A sample in which the total moves backwards is
  skipped rather than plotted as a spike.


*****************************************************************************
  Note:  the AIX 4.x back end reads /dev/kmem, which is mode 0440 and
    owned by root:system, so on those releases xosview must either run as
    root or be installed setgid system.  Without that access it starts,
    reports the problem on stderr and shows every meter as disabled.  The
    AIX 5.x back end needs no privileges.
*****************************************************************************
